        "example_2": "",
        "antonym": "abandhanokāsa",
        "synonym": "",
        "variant": "",
        "var_phonetic": "",
        "var_text": "",
        "commentary": "(KVt) bandhan'okāse sati vā asati vā bandhanavirahito patto abandhano, pañcabandhanānaṃ okāso assā'ti <b>pañcabandhan'okāso</b>.",
        "notes": "",
        "cognate": "",
        "link": "",
        "family_set": "",
        "origin": "",
        "stem": "bandhanokās",
        "pattern": "a adj",
        "comment": "since rāji here is 1.3 - crack; and because in the Vibhanga in Permutations bandhanokāsa does not present, when abandhanokāsa is present to count as a full offence - the meaning here - An almsbowl with a mend that counts; From BMC: “The Vibhaṅga to the following rule says that a bhikkhu may ask for a new bowl if his current bowl has five mends or more, the space for a mend (§) being two inches (fingerbreadths). The Commentary explains this first by saying that a bowl with five mends or more is damaged beyond repair, and thus loses its determination as a bowl. It then expands on the Vibhaṅga’s statements as follows: A clay bowl is damaged beyond repair if it has at least ten inches of cracks in it, the smallest of the cracks being at least two inches long. Cracks less than two inches long are said not to merit mending—this is the meaning of the Vibhaṅga’s phrase, “space for a mend”—and so do not count."
    },
